Consider the following Dirichlet problem for the $p$-Laplace equation in a sector $\Sigma$:

$$
\begin{cases}
\mathrm{div} (|\nabla u|^{p-2} \nabla u) = 0 & \text{in} \; \Sigma, \\
u =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{on} \; \partial_B \Sigma, \\
0 & \text{on} \; \partial_A \cup \partial_{N}\Sigma,
\end{cases}
\end{cases}
$$

where $\partial_B \Sigma$ represents the flat boundary of $\Sigma$. Assuming $p \neq 1$, we investigate the behavior of the $p$-harmonic measure of a ball of radius $r$ centered at a point on the flat boundary, with respect to a point located at a distance $r$ perpendicularly from the center, from the limit as $r$ approaches zero, yielding a non-zero constant. Additionally, it is shown that the problem exhibits a phase transition at $p = 2$ when the exponent of the distance function in the polar coordinates system is non-negative.

